WEBVTT

00:01.200 --> 00:06.212
This is ADLC, the Agentic Delivery Life Cycle.

00:06.462 --> 00:18.360
It turns your idea into a product spec, an architecture and a backlog of GitHub issues. Then it delivers those issues, one by one, as tested and reviewed pull requests.

00:18.610 --> 00:24.674
In this video you'll set it up, start a first project, and see how to work with it day to day.

00:26.224 --> 00:27.464
There are two halves.

00:27.714 --> 00:37.659
First, you plan with Claude: you describe the product, agree on an architecture, and Claude turns it into GitHub issues with their dependencies.

00:37.909 --> 00:45.341
Then the agents deliver, unattended. A poller on your dev server picks up ready issues in dependency order.

00:45.591 --> 00:58.739
For each one, a team of agents writes the spec, the design and the tests first, builds the code, verifies it, runs end-to-end checks and reviews it, then opens a pull request.

00:58.989 --> 01:07.393
Low- and medium-risk changes merge by themselves once CI is green. High-risk changes wait for your approval.

01:08.943 --> 01:10.154
Here's what you need.

01:10.404 --> 01:16.233
A Claude subscription. Max is recommended, because unattended runs use it.

01:16.483 --> 01:20.048
A GitHub account, with the gh command line.

01:20.298 --> 01:34.607
And a Linux server with Docker: your dev server. It runs the agents, their test environments, the CI runners and a dashboard. Give it about twenty-four gigabytes of memory and fifty gigabytes of free disk.

01:35.807 --> 01:37.909
Part one: install.

01:39.359 --> 01:47.027
On the dev server, clone the ADLC repository, link the adlc command, and create its data folder.

01:47.277 --> 01:55.989
Then run adlc doctor. It checks Docker, disk space and virtualisation, and tells you what's missing.

01:57.539 --> 02:03.282
The agents need their own identity on GitHub, so you create a GitHub App, once.

02:03.532 --> 02:13.053
Give it read and write access to contents, issues, pull requests and workflows, and read access to actions and commit statuses. Nothing else.

02:13.303 --> 02:17.534
Install it on your project's repository, and generate a private key.

02:17.784 --> 02:25.373
Because the agents act as the app, you review and approve their pull requests like a colleague's, and they can never push to main.

02:26.923 --> 02:32.412
Now create the project: an empty repository, then its secrets on the dev server.

02:32.673 --> 02:39.320
The secrets stay on the server, readable only by you. Claude never sees their values.

02:39.570 --> 02:42.396
Start the runner, and connect the GitHub App.

02:43.770 --> 02:50.940
Log in to Claude once, and enable Remote Control, so you can talk to Claude on the server from your laptop or your phone.

02:51.190 --> 02:54.558
Finally, start the dashboard, and get its login token.

02:55.758 --> 02:58.373
Part two: plan your product.

02:59.823 --> 03:10.776
Open the Claude app, or claude.ai/code, and pick the session adlc-orbit. Now you're talking to Claude on the dev server.

03:11.026 --> 03:25.721
Run /adlc-core:init. Claude goes through the setup section by section: repository, CI, registry, environments, and what the agents may do on their own.

03:25.971 --> 03:32.768
Each proposal comes with a reason. You confirm it, change it, or leave it to be decided later.

03:33.018 --> 03:44.806
It writes adlc.yaml and a setup document, and a provisioning script that you run yourself, with your own GitHub login, because the agents have no admin rights.

03:46.356 --> 03:51.630
Next, describe your idea with /adlc-core:discover.

03:51.880 --> 03:57.929
Claude asks questions and writes a product requirements document with testable acceptance criteria.

03:58.179 --> 04:05.057
Then /adlc-core:architect designs the system and records each decision as an architecture decision record.

04:05.307 --> 04:20.869
And /adlc-core:plan-backlog breaks the work into epics and issues, with milestones and dependencies. The first issue is always a walking skeleton: one thin path from screen to API to database, with CI and a test environment.

04:21.119 --> 04:26.941
Each step ends with a pull request of its documents, so everything is versioned in your repository.

04:28.141 --> 04:30.784
Part three: watch it deliver.

04:32.234 --> 04:38.291
Open the dashboard. The Queue tab lists every open issue in the order the agents will take them.

04:38.541 --> 04:44.305
Issues wait for their blockers. The waitlist can only start once booking is merged.

04:44.555 --> 04:50.673
Orange items are your own tasks, like creating a secret. They unblock the issues behind them.

04:50.923 --> 04:53.884
Run next moves an issue to the front of the line.

04:55.434 --> 05:01.458
The Dependencies tab draws the same queue as a graph, so you can see what is holding up what.

05:03.008 --> 05:05.803
The Now tab shows what's happening right now.

05:06.053 --> 05:12.992
Issue 18 is being delivered: the agent is in the Build phase, step four of eight.

05:13.242 --> 05:20.874
The pull request for issue 17 is in the merge train, waiting for its CI checks, and you can see which jobs are running.

05:21.124 --> 05:27.122
Below, the dev deployment waits for the newest images to be published, with a link to that run.

05:27.372 --> 05:33.061
Further down is the run itself: its phases, and a live log of what the agents are doing.

05:33.311 --> 05:46.228
Each run works in its own git worktree and test environment, and tests what the change affects. The full suite runs in the pull request's CI, which must be green before anything merges.

05:47.778 --> 05:51.274
When the agents need you, it shows up under Needs you.

05:51.524 --> 05:59.742
At the top, the merge train. It merges pull requests one after another, bringing each up to date and checking it first.

05:59.992 --> 06:12.114
High-risk changes, like payments, wait for your review. The pull request explains the spec, the risk, which test covers each acceptance criterion, and the review verdicts.

06:12.364 --> 06:19.078
Questions land here too. Answer on the issue, relabel it ready, and the run continues where it stopped.

06:19.328 --> 06:26.501
And issues the agents opened, such as a flaky test, don't start until you press Start or Run next.

06:27.701 --> 06:30.197
Part four: day to day.

06:31.647 --> 06:37.806
Day to day, ADLC runs mostly on its own. Start with the Overview.

06:38.056 --> 06:46.482
It shows each project's running delivery, the pull requests to review, blocked runs and your tasks, and how busy each machine is.

06:48.032 --> 06:56.531
Then go through Needs you. Review and approve the high-risk pull requests: you only approve, and the merge train does the merging.

06:56.781 --> 07:05.140
Do your owner tasks, like creating the secret a new feature needs. Close the task, and the issues it blocked start by themselves.

07:05.390 --> 07:07.387
And answer the agents' questions.

07:08.937 --> 07:20.130
If priorities change, use Run next on an issue, or on a whole epic in the Epics tab. For lasting changes, revise the backlog with Claude.

07:21.680 --> 07:29.706
The Health tab holds the switches: the merge train, reworks on failed checks, and how much the agents test during delivery.

07:29.956 --> 07:39.346
It shows the nightly checks, which run the exhaustive suites and open a regression issue when something breaks. You can also start them now.

07:39.596 --> 07:43.435
And the logs of the poller, the deployer and Remote Control.

07:44.985 --> 07:51.299
History lists every run with its duration and cost, so you always know what the agents spend.

07:52.849 --> 07:57.208
From a terminal, adlc status gives you the same picture.

07:57.458 --> 08:03.056
Pause stops new runs while the current one finishes, and resume starts them again.

08:03.306 --> 08:07.580
And adlc logs follows any component, or a single run.

08:09.130 --> 08:18.860
When something goes wrong, the agents rework their own pull requests: failed checks and merge conflicts go back to them, twice at most.

08:19.110 --> 08:26.711
After that, or when a run gets stuck, the issue is marked blocked or needs human review, with a summary of why.

08:26.961 --> 08:32.683
Fix the cause or answer the question, relabel the issue ready, and it continues.

08:32.933 --> 08:37.981
And the agents never deploy production: you deploy releases yourself.

08:39.531 --> 08:49.281
To learn more, open the Field Guide from the dashboard's sidebar. It has an issue lifecycle simulator, tutorials, and fixes for common problems.

08:50.831 --> 08:57.897
That's ADLC. Describe what you want, plan it with Claude, and let the agents deliver it.

08:58.147 --> 09:02.801
You'll find everything on GitHub, at kororos/adlc.
